== block 3: Proposition 6.4 (additive work)
  class: all functions [K] -> {0,1}; L calls at independent uniform inputs; good run: at most m wrong answers.
  protocol D_a: ask a distinct inputs, answer them correctly and every other call by a fair coin;
  then the number of wrong answers is Bin(L, (K-a)/(2K)) for every member.
     K     L    m  delta | bound K(1-2m/L-2delta) | least a with failure <= delta | failure at a = ceil(bound)-1
     20    20    2   0.10 |                   12.0 |                            18 | 0.7939
    200   100   10   0.10 |                  120.0 |                           172 | 0.9943
    200   100   10   0.01 |                  156.0 |                           181 | 0.5784
    400  1000  100   0.05 |                  280.0 |                           332 | 1.0000
     83    50    5   0.10 |                   49.8 |                            73 | 0.9594
   1000    10    1   0.10 |                  600.0 |                           891 | 0.6242
  (every row: the least number of questions of D_a is at least the bound, as the proposition requires
   of every protocol; with one question fewer than the bound the failure of D_a exceeds delta)
  theta = delta = 0.1: the factor 1 - 2 theta - 2 delta = 0.6; 50 answers serve K <= 50/0.6 = 83.3 bits
  exhaustive, K = 4, L = 3, m = 0: for each set S of asked inputs, the largest probability of a good run
  that a deployed answerer can have on average over the 16 functions, against 1 - [L(K-|S|)/(2K) - m]/(L - m):
    |S| = 0: average probability of a good run 0.2188;  the proof's cap 0.5000
    |S| = 1: average probability of a good run 0.3320;  the proof's cap 0.6250
    |S| = 2: average probability of a good run 0.4922;  the proof's cap 0.7500
    |S| = 3: average probability of a good run 0.7109;  the proof's cap 0.8750
    |S| = 4: average probability of a good run 1.0000;  the proof's cap 1.0000

